Title: The Crescent
Order: 3
Production Code: 1x03

EXT. MAGNOLIA FARM, BACK PIAZZA - NOON (MAY)

PAGE: 1

PANEL: The same back piazza. Colonel Titus smokes in his favorite seat, a julep at his elbow. Two riders come up the avenue: THOMSON in front, and behind him a slender, well-knit stranger in a good riding coat, blue-eyed, golden-haired: WARREN MAXWELL.

CAPTION: It was still the pleasant month of May.

PANEL: Thomson reins in at the foot of the steps, waving a hand toward the stranger like a man producing a rabbit.

THOMSON
I've brought you a visitor, Colonel, a stranger and yet not a stranger, bein' as we've met before. He brings you news.

PANEL: Warren in the saddle, his face pleasant and perfectly blank; the Colonel rising, hand out.

THOMSON (CONT'D)
Mr. Maxwell, Colonel Titus. Mr. Maxwell has come all the way from London to bring you news from the Hall.

COLONEL TITUS
That's the right sound, William. Mr. Maxwell, do you stop with us over night, sir?

PANEL: Warren steps up to the piazza, taking off his hat.

WARREN
I fear I must tax your hospitality to that extent.

WARREN (CONT'D)
The estate will be yours in one year if the direct heir is not found.

PAGE: 2

PANEL: The Colonel waves Warren to a chair; a pitcher of julep on the table between them.

COLONEL TITUS
You remember Mr. Pendleton? Old Pen, we boys used to call him.

COLONEL TITUS (CONT'D)
I hope he'll continue to look after my affairs, if the estate comes to me.

PANEL: Warren bows, smiling, hands folded.

WARREN
There is very little doubt of your succeeding to the baronetcy.

WARREN (CONT'D)
This is my second trip to America in two years, hunting up the Carlingford heirs.

PANEL: Warren, cigar in hand, with the look of a man who has been told bad news before.

WARREN (CONT'D)
I thought I had found Lord George's younger son, Henry, on my first trip,

WARREN (CONT'D)
but after a fruitless chase I was forced to give it up.

WARREN (CONT'D)
We are convinced that he is dead and without issue.

PANEL: The Colonel looks into his glass; for a second his eyes go to Thomson and back.

COLONEL TITUS
Poor Henry! His was a sad fate. But it was his destiny. Do you believe in destiny, my young friend?

PANEL: Warren, quiet and earnest.

WARREN
I believe that many things we call destiny may be overcome by resolving to conquer difficulties, not allowing them to conquer us.

PAGE: 3

PANEL: The Colonel calls over his shoulder toward the hallway.

COLONEL TITUS
Jude! Jude, I say!

PANEL: Judah steps out of the hall, a big shape in the doorway, in a clean cotton shirt, hat in hand.

PANEL: Close on Warren, who starts involuntarily: a tiny movement of the shoulders and then nothing; his eyes on Judah and a flash of the island in them.

PANEL: Thomson and the Colonel, side by side, exchange a glance that the camera catches and Warren does not; both with half-smiles.

PANEL: Judah's impassive face as the Colonel gives him an order; nothing in it, not a muscle moving.

COLONEL TITUS
Take the gentleman's horse, and tell Mrs. Thomson we have a guest over night.

PAGE: 4

PANEL: The Colonel lights a pipe and squints at Warren.

COLONEL TITUS
Thomson, it appears to me that I have seen Mr. Maxwell before. What do you say?

PANEL: Thomson leans back in his chair, enjoying himself.

THOMSON
I reckon you have. Don' you remember our hunting trip up at Erie two years ago? And the murder of White Eagle?

PANEL: The Colonel snaps his fingers, with a show of recollection.

COLONEL TITUS
Sure enough! Mr. Maxwell was the young Englishman who took such a prominent part in the affair.

PANEL: The Colonel, with a keen glance at Warren's quiet face.

COLONEL TITUS (CONT'D)
Strange, too, that the man should have been killed just when the children needed him most.

COLONEL TITUS (CONT'D)
If he had lived, Thomson, in all probability, would not have recovered his property.

PANEL: Warren puffs at his cigar as though it were his only object in life.

WARREN
Then Mr. Thomson was the owner of the unfortunate children?

THOMSON
I'd been hunting them gals and their mother for nigh fifteen years.

PAGE: 5

PANEL: Thomson takes a long pull at his drink and turns to Warren, easy and amiable.

THOMSON
What do you think of our institutions, Mr. Maxwell? 'Spose you have some compunctions of conscience over us, eh? Most Englishmen do at first.

PANEL: Warren, dry, one eyebrow up.

WARREN
Yet you complained of a tea tax, and fought a "liberty fight" on that pretext.

PANEL: Thomson, unabashed, waves the cigar.

THOMSON
See what we've done for the Africans, given them the advantages of Christian training. The niggers have decidedly the best of it.

PANEL: Warren puts his glass down, firm.

WARREN
My opinion is that you are wrong. A government cannot prosper founded on crushed and helpless humanity.

PANEL: The Colonel, soothing, holding up a hand.

COLONEL TITUS
There are two sides to every question. Each group of men in this country has its own standard of right and wrong.

PAGE: 6

PANEL: The Colonel leans forward, eyes shifting, voice low and flat.

COLONEL TITUS
We won't give our ideas up for no d----d greasy, Northern mechanic.

PANEL: A bell rings at the house. Warren stands, relieved.

CAPTION: The announcement that dinner was served cut short further discussion, much to Warren's relief.

PANEL: Late afternoon on the plantation, Warren riding beside the Colonel through the cotton rows; field hands bending; an overseer's whip on a post.

CAPTION: The remainder of the day was spent studying the beauties of the "institution" as propounded by the philosophical Colonel.

PANEL: A lawn in the evening sun: Winona, in a gray cotton gown with her hair cropped short, pushes Lillian's rolling chair up and down. Warren, at the far end of the frame on horseback, sees her and does not turn his head.

CAPTION: Once only, Warren's anxious gaze descried Winona wheeling the chair of her crippled mistress.

PANEL: Night on the piazza. Warren alone with a cigar in the soft dark; something blacker than the night moves behind him and a low voice speaks without stopping.

JUDAH (O.P.)
Leave your window unlatched.

PAGE: 7

INT. MAGNOLIA FARM, WARREN'S ROOM - NIGHT

PANEL: Warren lies on the bed in his clothes with a pistol on the stand; the window slightly open; a candle gone out.

PANEL: Close on his face, eyes open, listening: the sound of drums in the distance, marching feet, then females weeping in the house, then nothing.

CAPTION: His excited fancy could detect the sound of drums and the hurrying of marching feet.

PANEL: About one o'clock. A big shape beside the bed, a hand on Warren's shoulder; moonlight on the dark face of Judah.

SFX: SHH

PANEL: Judah strips his shirt up over his back in the moonlight; hundreds of scars, crossed and crossed again. Warren's hand pressed gently over them.

JUDAH
I could bear it all, Mr. Maxwell. But Winona.

PANEL: Judah, his voice breaking.

JUDAH (CONT'D)
They've educated her to increase her value in the slave market, and next week Mr.

JUDAH (CONT'D)
Thomson takes her and me up the river to sell us to the highest bidder.

PAGE: 8

PANEL: Judah, steady, grim; Warren's face white in the dark.

JUDAH
If help does not come I have sworn to kill her before she shall become slavery's victim.

PANEL: Warren puts his arm round the boy's neck and shoulders.

WARREN
No extremes, Judah, until all else fails.

WARREN (CONT'D)
I can buy you both if it comes to that, and my promise to take you to England with me still holds good.

PANEL: Judah, quiet and urgent, a finger to his lips.

JUDAH
I doubt that you will be allowed to buy us. There is a stronger reason for our destruction underlying all this than is apparent.

PANEL: Close on Judah's face in profile, half moon, half shadow.

JUDAH (CONT'D)
Don't let it be known that we have held any communication with you. Be cautious.

PANEL: Warren, tense, leaning in.

WARREN
I hardly know how to meet this issue if the use of money is denied us. When do you leave?

PAGE: 9

PANEL: Judah's answer, short.

JUDAH
Monday, on the "Crescent."

PANEL: Warren nods, decided.

WARREN
Then I'll plead pressing business and leave to-morrow to meet you on board the steamer when she sails.

WARREN (CONT'D)
Trust me, Judah, I will not fail you.

PANEL: Judah, tears in his throat, his hand over Warren's.

JUDAH
I do trust you, Mr. Maxwell, next to God.

JUDAH (CONT'D)
I dreamt a year ago that I saw you coming toward me out of a cloud of intense blackness.

PANEL: Judah, bitter, looking away at the window and the stars.

JUDAH (CONT'D)
Only for my hope in you as our deliverer, I'd have shot myself months ago.

PANEL: Warren, solemn.

WARREN
There is a God, Judah.

JUDAH
But He seems far off from my unfortunate race.

PANEL: They part as silently as they met; Judah's big shape slides out of the window frame and is gone, the curtain stirring.

WARREN
With God's aid, I will save you or sacrifice myself.

EXT. KANSAS CITY WHARF - AFTERNOON

PAGE: 10

PANEL: The steamer Crescent at her moorings on the Missouri, black smoke, white paint, a gang-plank, wooden piles, a pile of lumber on the wharf; all the bustle of a river landing.

CAPTION: The steamer "Crescent" tugged and pulled at her moorings as if impatient of delay.

PANEL: A strange figure comes down the gang-plank: buckskin breeches suspended by one strap over a flannel shirt, high boots, a battered hat pushed back from a ruddy round face, a belt bristling with pistols and bowie knives. Warren, at the rail, throws away his cigar.

PANEL: Warren holds out his hand; the man turns, smiling, and grasps it in a mighty clasp: EBENEZER MAYBEE.

WARREN
Mr. Maybee, of Erie?

MAYBEE
Dog my cats, ef it ain't Mr. Maxwell! I'm pow'ful glad to meet you ag'in.

PANEL: Maybee, rueful, takes his hat off and wipes his face.

MAYBEE (CONT'D)
Poor old White Eagle! Nary sound have I heard, Mr. Maxwell, since, of them unfortoonit children neither.

PANEL: Maybee, softly.

MAYBEE (CONT'D)
I'm right chicken-hearted when I wake up at night, an' think about the leetle gal, po' pretty critter!

PAGE: 11

PANEL: Warren, hot with shame, a fist at his own chest.

WARREN
I feel like a miserable cur whenever I think how supinely I have rested while such a horror was perpetrated.

WARREN (CONT'D)
May I have a word with you in private?

PANEL: Maybee looks round cautiously at the deck full of gamblers and slave-traders.

MAYBEE
Hu--sh! Them ar sentimen's breathes pizen in this loorid atmosphere.

PANEL: Maybee points at the pile of lumber on the wharf.

MAYBEE (CONT'D)
See that pile o' lumber out on the wharf?

MAYBEE (CONT'D)
That's the best place I know on to have a leetle private conversation with a friend.

PANEL: The two men sitting on the ends of the lumber pile, Maybee with a knife and a whittling stick, keeping a sharp lookout; behind them the steamer's smoke.

WARREN
There'll be a war in this country in less than two years, I predict.

MAYBEE
No need o' waitin' two years, mister; jes' make it two months.

PANEL: Maybee, grinning, leaning in.

MAYBEE (CONT'D)
John Brown's calling for volunteers an' I'm boun' to be in the fight.

MAYBEE (CONT'D)
It beats all nature how fightin' jes' grows on a man once he's had a taste.

PAGE: 12

PANEL: Maybee points a thumb at the steamer, voice flat.

MAYBEE
The Rangers came over from this side under that devil, Bill Thomson,

MAYBEE (CONT'D)
an' took Bud Wilson's ol' woman inter the woods because of a letter she writ home.

PANEL: Warren, shuddering, hand over his mouth.

MAYBEE (CONT'D)
I won't pain yer feelin's by recountering the rest.

WARREN
My God!

PANEL: Maybee whittles, still looking at the water.

MAYBEE
They killed Capt. R. P. Brown at Easton; Bill himself leaned over the po' man an' squirted terbacco juice into his eyes.

PANEL: Warren, with flashing eyes and a straight back.

WARREN
I'm a pretty good shot, Mr. Maybee.

WARREN (CONT'D)
After I finish this matter for my firm,

WARREN (CONT'D)
I should like nothing better than to put myself and my pistols at the disposal of Mr.

WARREN (CONT'D)
Brown.

PANEL: Maybee spits a stream of tobacco juice, and smooths the end of his board to his entire satisfaction.

MAYBEE
Volunteers is ac-ceptable, certainly, ef they brings weapins and ammunition. Now, what's the business you wanted to lay befo' me?

PAGE: 13

PANEL: Warren, trembling, tells it; Maybee chewing and spitting like an automaton.

MAYBEE
Sho now! Want ter know!

PANEL: Maybee rears up, shocked and furious.

MAYBEE
That ar Thomson, too! Dad gum 'im fer an onery skunk! I've jes' got to kill 'im; can't help it!

PANEL: Maybee jabs at the sky with the whittling stick.

MAYBEE (CONT'D)
We must git 'em on the Underground railroad this night.

MAYBEE (CONT'D)
You go with the boat an' I'll cut across country an' com--moonicate with Parson Steward.

PANEL: Maybee looks up at the clouds mounting over the river.

MAYBEE (CONT'D)
O Lord, ef we'd only git such a thunder storm as we had the night White Eagle was murdered,

MAYBEE (CONT'D)
it'd be the makin' of this expe--dition.

PANEL: Maybee giving the last instructions, one finger up; Warren nodding.

MAYBEE (CONT'D)
Tell Judah to git Thomson drunk. Then git ashore somehow at Weston, an' I'll meet you there with hosses.

PAGE: 14

PANEL: The gang-plank. Thomson comes aboard first, hat tipped, with a pistol in his belt, followed by Judah and Winona in the middle of a file of other slaves. Even the rude passengers on the deck turn to look at her.

CAPTION: Just before the last bell rang, Thomson came aboard with his slaves.

PANEL: Winona crosses the deck, hair short and clustered in a rich plume about her neck, close-fitting cotton gown, eyes large with anxiety; her step firm; she bears herself like a young princess.

CAPTION: Even the rude passengers were moved by the beauty of the slave girl.

PANEL: Warren in a corner of the deck, watching; she throws one hurried, timid glance in his direction and it hits him like a hand on the chest.

PANEL: The slave pen on the deck beside an air-shaft; Judah sits down on the planks, the others around him.

PANEL: Warren's stateroom port-hole opening directly beside Judah's seat; a hand with a folded note held out.

CAPTION: A note was easily slipped to the slave telling him of Mr. Maybee's plan.

PAGE: 15

INT. THE CRESCENT, CABIN - NIGHT

PANEL: The long cabin: gas-lit, tables, a bar, fifteen young Virginians in shirt-sleeves; and in the middle of the floor an open coffin on trestles. Thomson at the center of the table with a bottle.

CAPTION: One of their number had been fatally shot in a quarrel at an hotel in Kansas City; they were carrying the body home.

PANEL: A young Virginian raising a toast to the coffin; the rest roaring with laughter.

CAPTION: They had ordered the coffin brought in and placed in the center of the cabin, where, as they said, the poor fellow might have the comfort of witnessing one more good time.

PANEL: The men standing on chairs singing with their arms round one another's necks.

VIRGINIANS
You Yankees tremble, and abolitionists fall!

VIRGINIANS (CONT'D)
Our motto is Southern Rights for all!

PANEL: Thomson, the most reckless at the table, a card in one hand and a glass in the other, slurring.

PANEL: Thomson stretched on the floor beside a table, a Virginian asleep on the coffin lid, bottles everywhere. The few women passengers are shown fastened in their staterooms behind a locked door.

CAPTION: Soon he, and the rest of the party, was stretched in a drunken stupor from which nothing could arouse them.

PAGE: 16

EXT. THE CRESCENT, DECK - NIGHT

PANEL: Warren with saddle-bags, picking his way among the sleeping men; the sky black, the first heavy drops starting. Behind him the boat shudders.

PANEL: The steamer grinds to a stop on a sandbar, listing; officers on the wheel-deck shouting, deckhands running.

SFX: SKRRRRRRK

CAPTION: Half-way to Weston they had struck upon a sand-bar and there they hung, shuddering and groaning in the teeth of the storm.

PANEL: Warren seats himself near the railing in the downpour. A soft touch on his arm; Judah crouched in the shadow of a mast.

JUDAH
They won't be off this bar before morning. I'm going to drop a boat over the side the next heavy crash that comes.

PANEL: Close on Judah, whispering.

JUDAH (CONT'D)
Winona is waiting just back of you. It'll take nerve, but it is the only way. We must be silent and careful.

PANEL: Warren, alone again, his hand tightening; a soft hand steals into his. He does not turn his head; he knows without looking.

PAGE: 17 (SPLASH)

PANEL: Full-page splash, lightning-lit: the steamer's high white side over a black boiling river. Warren at the rail has swung Winona clear and holds her by the wrists over the water, her bare feet dangling; below them in the dark, Judah in a small boat tossed up on a wave, both arms up to catch her. The bolt has just struck the capstan behind them and the first mate is falling. Rain streaks everywhere.
LIGHT: Blue-white lightning, then dark again

CAPTION: Surely God was with them.

SFX: KRAAAKOOOM

JUDAH
All right; let her drop!

PAGE: 18

PANEL: Close on Warren's hands letting go.

PANEL: Winona falling through the dark, her hair streaming.

SFX: SPLASH

PANEL: Judah in the boat, standing upright, catching her in his arms as deftly as a ball is caught and tossed between players.

CAPTION: His Indian training in managing canoes made him fearless now, and his giant strength served him well.

PANEL: Warren lets himself down the ropes hand over hand, swings clear, and lands lightly in the middle of the frail craft, steadied by the giant black.

JUDAH
All right; come ahead.

PANEL: The three of them in the shadow of the great hull until another lightning flash has passed. Then each man settles an oar into a rowlock.

PANEL: Wide on the black river: the steamer a bright wall of lights growing small on the right, the little boat a dark dot on the water; Judah pushing off into the night.
